      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND/AIMS",
          "content": "This study evaluated usefulness of an abdominal bandage for colonoscopy which enables proper compression of the abdomen during the entire examination."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODOLOGY",
          "content": "Patients undergoing colonoscopy were subjected to either traditional methods or using an abdominal bandage. Two hundred and twelve patients were divided into two groups: group A (colonoscopy using traditional methods) and group B (colonoscopy using the abdominal bandage). After the examination, each patient completed a form that assessed the degree of pain during the procedure (no pain, mild, moderate, severe)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There was no significant difference in total colonoscopy completion rate between the two groups. With regard to the degree of pain, the results for groups A and B were, respectively: no pain= 10.3% and 14.3%, mild = 29.0% and 51.4%, moderate = 48.6% and 27.6%, severe=12.1% and 6.7% (P=0.03)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The abdominal bandage decreased the degree of patient pain compared with traditional methods."
        }
      ]},
